  "query_preview": "..Across central and northern Utah and southwest \nWyoming, temperatures are trending about 10 to 15 degrees cooler \ncompared to yesterday, thanks to a dry cold front that passed \nthrough the area. Post-frontal northwest flow across the region\ntoday has resulted in an influx of both vertically integrated and near-\nsurface smoke originating from numerous fires across the Pacific \nNorthwest, leading to deteriorated visibility and air quality\nacross much of the area. High-resolution guidance indicates this\nsmoke will continue to advect into northern Utah and southwest\nWyoming through at least Tuesday, with central Utah continuing to\nsee impacts from the Widemouth 2 Fire as fire activity continues. \n\nAcross far southern and eastern Utah, the dry cold front resulted\nin very little change to temperatures, with the main impact being\nincreased west to northwest winds that have combined with very \nlow relative humidity to create critical fire weather conditions \nacross these areas. Winds taper across far southern Utah on \nTuesday, but are expected to remain elevated across eastern Utah \nincluding the San Rafael Swell and western Uinta Basin, keeping an\nelevated threat for critical fire weather conditions in place. \n\nHigh pressure redevelops across the western US around mid-week,\nwith a ridge amplifying across the Great Basin Friday into\nSaturday. This pattern will favor redevelopment of widespread\ndangerous heat for the latter portion of the week, with continued\ndry conditions outside of some isolated convection along the high\nterrain of southern Utah. Widespread Major to locally Extreme\nHeatRisk is expected to develop Friday and Saturday as\ntemperatures peak across the region. Warm overnight lows will also\noffer little relief, which yields heightened concerns for heat-\nrelated illness. \n\nThe area of high pressure becomes centered over the Four Corners\nlate in the weekend, resulting in a modest monsoonal surge into \nthe region into early next week. As such, the threat for heavy\nrainfall will increase as early as Sunday as moisture deepens,\nmainly across portions of central and southern Utah.",
